  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Georgia totaled $7.5B in FY2026 to date. Award activity reached 68,698 actions with an average award size of $109K. Obligations fell 35.9% year over year. Historical context shows variable annual totals: FY21 was $11.1B, FY22 was $8.5B, FY23 was $11.1B, FY24 was $10.4B, and FY25 was $11.6B. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in Georgia, obligating $4.1B. The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $1.1B and the General Services Administration obligated $805M. LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP leads vendors with $637M, followed by MANHATTAN TORCON A JOINT VENTURE at $492M and GEORGIA TECH APPLIED RESEARCH CORP at $246M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $966M, followed by Aircraft Manufacturing at $699M and Engineering Services at $622M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did Georgia’s federal obligations change year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 35.9% year over year. Annual totals show fluctuation across recent fiscal years, with FY25 at $11.6B and FY26 at $7.5B. Changes in obligations correspond to shifts in buying agency activity, led by the Department of Defense at $4.1B and other large buyers such as the Department of Health and Human Services at $1.1B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries dominate federal contracting in Georgia?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $4.1B in obligations. LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP is the largest vendor with $637M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry with $966M in obligations. Other major buyers include the Department of Health and Human Services at $1.1B and the General Services Administration at $805M."
      }
    ]
  },
